\mathcal{P}'=\\{(1,2),(1,3),(2,2)\\}\\).  \n  No two of these points have an admissible slope (the slopes are \\(\\infty\\), \\(0\\), or \\(-1\\)).  \n  Thus three sunny lines would be needed – contradiction.\n\n* **\\(y=2\\)** covers \\((1,2),(2,2)\\).  \n  \\(\\mathcal{P}'=\\{(1,1),(1,3),(2,1),(3,1)\\}\\).  \n  The point \\((1,1)\\) cannot share a sunny line with any other point of \\(\\mathcal{P}'\\) (all possible slopes are forbidden).  \n  Consequently at least three sunny lines are required – contradiction.\n\n* **\\(y=3\\)** covers \\((1,3)\\).  \n  \\(\\mathcal{P}'=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\\).  \n  The only admissible sunny pair is \\((1,1)\\) with \\((2,2)\\) (slope \\(1\\)).  \n  After using that line, the four remaining points have only forbidden slopes between any two of them, forcing more than two sunny lines – contradiction.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  \\(L_{0}\\) diagonal  \n\n* **\\(x+y=2\\)** covers \\((1,1)\\).  \n  \\(\\mathcal{P}'=\\{(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\\).  \n  The only admissible sunny pairs are \\((1,2)\\) with \\((3,1)\\) (slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\)) and \\((1,3)\\) with \\((2,1)\\) (slope \\(-2\\)).  \n  After using those two lines, the point \\((2,2)\\) remains uncovered, requiring a third sunny line – contradiction.\n\n* **\\(x+y=3\\)** covers \\((1,2),(2,1)\\).  \n  \\(\\mathcal{P}'=\\{(1,1),(1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\\).  \n  The only admissible sunny pair among these is \\((1,1)\\) with \\((2,2)\\) (slope \\(1\\)).  \n  The remaining points \\((1,3)\\) and \\((3,1)\\) lie on the line \\(x+y=4\\), whose slope is \\(-1\\) (non‑sunny).  \n  Hence they cannot be covered by a sunny line, and a third line would be necessary – contradiction.\n\n* **\\(x+y=4\\)** covers \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\).  \n  \\(\\mathcal{P}'=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \n  As in the horizontal case \\(y=1\\), no two of these three points have an admissible slope; three sunny lines would be required – contradiction.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nSince every possible choice for the unique non‑sunny line leads to a contradiction, **no** set of three distinct lines can satisfy the covering condition while having exactly two sunny lines.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For }n=3\\text{, a configuration with }k=2\\text{ sunny lines does not exist.}}\n\\]"
