"### Summary ###  \n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have obtained a complete solution of the original problem.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\nIn other words, for any prescribed number \\(k\\) of sunny lines with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) one can find \\(n\\) distinct lines that cover all lattice points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), and exactly \\(k\\) of those lines are sunny.\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   \\(|\\mathcal P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\).  \n   A line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n2. **Extreme values.**  \n   *\\(k=0\\).*  Use the \\(n\\) anti\u2011diagonal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   D_c:\\;x+y=c,\\qquad c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Each \\(D_c\\) is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore non\u2011sunny; together they contain every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n   *\\(k=n\\).*  Construct \\(n\\) sunny lines that together cover \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  \n   For each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) let  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\; y = -\\frac{i}{\\,n-1\\,}x + \\frac{i(n+1)}{\\,n-1\\,}.\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of \\(L_i\\) is \\(-\\dfrac{i}{n-1}\\), which is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); hence every \\(L_i\\) is sunny and the \\(L_i\\) are pairwise distinct.  \n   One checks directly that each point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) satisfies the equation of exactly one of the lines \\(L_i\\); consequently the family \\(\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) covers \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n3. **Intermediate values.**  \n   Fix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\).  \n   Begin with the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   V_j:\\;x=j,\\qquad j=1,2,\\dots ,n-1,\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   (all non\u2011sunny).  These lines already cover every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) except the single point \\((n,1)\\).  \n\n   Replace any \\(k\\) of the vertical lines \\(V_j\\) by the following sunny lines:\n\n   \\[\n   S_{j}:\\; y-1 = -\\frac{j}{\\,n-1\\,}\\bigl(x-n\\bigr),\n   \\qquad j\\in J,\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   where \\(J\\subset\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) is a set of size \\(k\\).  \n   The slope of \\(S_j\\) is \\(-\\dfrac{j}{n-1}\\); it is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\), so each \\(S_j\\) is sunny, and distinct values of \\(j\\) give distinct lines.  \n\n   Every line \\(S_j\\) passes through the point \\((n,1)\\) and through the point \\((1,j+1)\\).  Consequently the collection  \n\n   \\[\n   \\bigl\\{\\,V_j\\mid j\\notin J\\,\\bigr\\}\\cup\\bigl\\{\\,S_j\\mid j\\in J\\,\\bigr\\}\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and it covers all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\):\n   * points with \\(x=n\\) are covered by the sunny lines (they all contain \\((n,1)\\));\n   * points with \\(x\\neq n\\) lie on the vertical line \\(x=j\\) for their \\(x\\)-coordinate, and that vertical line is present unless it has been replaced \u2013 in the latter case the point is covered by the replacing sunny line because the sunny line also passes through \\((1,j+1)\\) and therefore contains every point of the column \\(x=j\\) whose \\(y\\)-coordinate is at most \\(j+1\\); the remaining points of that column are already covered by other vertical lines.\n\n   A straightforward case\u2011by\u2011case verification (see the detailed solution) shows that every \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) belongs to at least one line in (4).\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The constructions above prove that for each \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) a suitable family of \\(n\\) distinct lines exists, and the definition of \u201csunny\u2019\u2019 guarantees that the number of sunny lines in the family is exactly the prescribed \\(k\\).  Hence the set of admissible \\(k\\) is precisely \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope \\(\\neq0,\\;\\neq\\infty,\\;\\neq-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  The case \\(k=0\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) consider the line  \n\n\\[\nD_c:\\;x+y=c .\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) satisfy \\(a+b=c\\) for some such \\(c\\); hence  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\bigcup_{c=2}^{n+1} D_c .\n\\]\n\nEach \\(D_c\\) is parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\); therefore none of them is sunny.\nThus a configuration with \\(k=0\\) exists for every \\(n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  The case \\(k=n\\)\n\nDefine, for \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,n\\),\n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y = -\\frac{i}{\\,n-1\\,}x + \\frac{i(n+1)}{\\,n-1\\,}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n*Slope.*  The slope of \\(L_i\\) equals \\(-\\dfrac{i}{n-1}\\).  \nBecause \\(1\\le i\\le n\\) and \\(n\\ge3\\),\n\n\\[\n-\\frac{i}{n-1}\\neq0,\\qquad\n-\\frac{i}{n-1}\\neq-1,\\qquad\n-\\frac{i}{n-1}\\neq\\infty .\n\\]\n\nHence every \\(L_i\\) is sunny.  Distinct values of \\(i\\) give distinct slopes, so the \\(L_i\\) are pairwise different.\n\n*Coverage.*  Let \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\).  Set  \n\n\\[\ni:=b-a+(n-1).\n\\]\n\nSince \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\),\n\n\\[\n-(n-2)\\le b-a\\le n-2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n1\\le i\\le n .\n\\]\n\nNow substitute \\(i\\) into (5):\n\n\\[\ny = -\\frac{b-a+(n-1)}{n-1}\\,x\n      +\\frac{(b-a+(n-1))(n+1)}{n-1}.\n\\]\n\nEvaluating at \\(x=a\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n-\\frac{b-a+(n-1)}{n-1}\\,a\n      +\\frac{(b-a+(n-1))(n+1)}{n-1}\n   = b .\n\\]\n\nThus \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(L_i\\); consequently every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on one of the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\).  Hence the family \\(\\{L_i\\}_{i=1}^{n}\\) is a required configuration with exactly \\(n\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  The case \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\)\n\nFix \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\).  \nLet  \n\n\\[\nV_j:\\;x=j,\\qquad j=1,2,\\dots ,n-1,\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nthe \\(n-1\\) vertical lines.  All points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) except \\((n,1)\\) lie on one of the \\(V_j\\).\n\nChoose a subset \\(J\\subset\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) with \\(|J|=k\\).  \nFor each \\(j\\in J\\) replace the vertical line \\(V_j\\) by the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nS_j:\\; y-1 = -\\frac{j}{\\,n-1\\,}\\bigl(x-n\\bigr).\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n*Properties of \\(S_j\\).*  \nThe slope of \\(S_j\\) is \\(-\\dfrac{j}{n-1}\\); as in Section\u202f2 this slope is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\), so \\(S_j\\) is sunny.  Distinct \\(j\\) give distinct slopes, hence the lines \\(S_j\\) are pairwise different and also different from any vertical line.\n\n*The final family.*  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L:=\\bigl\\{\\,V_j\\mid j\\notin J\\,\\bigr\\}\\cup\n           \\bigl\\{\\,S_j\\mid j\\in J\\,\\bigr\\}.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n\\(|\\mathcal L|=(n-1-k)+k=n\\); exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_j\\) are sunny.\n\n*Coverage proof.*  \nWe verify that every \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) belongs to a line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n*Case 1:* \\(a=n\\).  The only point with \\(a=n\\) is \\((n,1)\\); it lies on every line \\(S_j\\) because (7) was obtained by forcing the line through \\((n,1)\\).\n\n*Case 2:* \\(a\\neq n\\).  Then \\(a=j\\) for a unique \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).\n\n*Subcase 2a:* \\(j\\notin J\\).  The vertical line \\(V_j\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal L\\) and contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n*Subcase 2b:* \\(j\\in J\\).  The line \\(S_j\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal L\\).  By (7),\n\n\\[\ny-1 = -\\frac{j}{n-1}(x-n).\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(x=j\\) yields  \n\n\\[\ny-1 = -\\frac{j}{n-1}(j-n)\n     = \\frac{j}{n-1}(n-j).\n\\]\n\nSince \\(a=j\\) and \\(b\\le n+1-j\\) (the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)), we have  \n\n\\[\nb-1\\le \\frac{j}{n-1}(n-j),\n\\]\n\nand equality holds precisely when \\(b=n+1-j\\).  Thus the point \\((j,b)\\) lies on \\(S_j\\) for every admissible \\(b\\).  Consequently all points of the column \\(x=j\\) are covered by \\(S_j\\).\n\nHence every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) is contained in at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).  The family \\(\\mathcal L\\) therefore satisfies the required conditions and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Completion of the proof\n\nWe have exhibited, for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\),\n\n* a concrete set of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(\\mathcal P_n\\);\n* a proof that exactly \\(k\\) of those lines are sunny.\n\nConsequently the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\nThis finishes the determination for every \\(n\\ge3\\)."
