_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   \\(|S_n|=n(n+1)/2\\).  \n\n2. **Base constructions (all lines non‑sunny).**  \n   *The \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\) cover \\(S_n\\); hence \\(k=0\\) is always possible.  \n\n3. **Upper bound for sunny lines.**  \n   *A sunny line cannot contain two points having the same abscissa, the same ordinate or the same sum of coordinates (otherwise it would be parallel to a forbidden direction).  \n   *Consequently a sunny line meets at most one point of each column \\(a\\) (and at most one of each row).  \n\n4. **Construction for arbitrary \\(k\\) when \\(n\\ge 4\\).**  \n   *Let \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) and put \\(m=n-k\\).  \n   *Use the \\(m\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   V_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,m)\n   \\]\n\n   (these are non‑sunny).  \n   *The remaining columns are \\(a=m+1,\\dots ,n\\); there are exactly \\(k\\) of them.  \n   *In column \\(a\\) the admissible ordinates are  \n\n   \\[\n   b\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,n+1-a\\},\n   \\]\n\n   and because \\(a\\ge m+1=n-k+1\\) we have \\(n+1-a\\le k\\).  \n   *Thus each of the last \\(k\\) columns contains **at most** \\(k\\) points, and we can assign the points of column \\(a\\) bijectively to the \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n\n   *For \\(t=1,\\dots ,k\\) define the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_t:\\;y = x-(m+t-1).\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(L_t\\) has slope \\(1\\) (hence is sunny) and passes through the points  \n\n   \\[\n   (m+t,1),\\;(m+t+1,2),\\dots ,(n,\\,n-m-t+1).\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   *Because \\(n-m-t+1 = k-t+1\\le k\\), every point listed in (2) indeed belongs to \\(S_n\\).  \n   *Collecting all \\(t\\) we see that the set of points covered by the \\(k\\) lines \\(\\{L_t\\}_{t=1}^{k}\\) is exactly the set of points of the columns \\(a=m+1,\\dots ,n\\).  \n\n   *Hence the family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\bigl\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_m,\\,L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\bigr\\}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of \