"### Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have obtained a complete solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1\\}, & n=3,\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,3,4,\\dots ,n\\}, & n\\ge 4 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\nIn other words, for \\(n\\ge4\\) every non\u2011negative integer \\(k\\) with \\(k\\neq2\\) and \\(k\\le n\\) can be realised; the value \\(k=2\\) is impossible for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   *A line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \n   *For a fixed \\(n\\) let  \n\n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   The set \\(T_n\\) contains \\(|T_n|=n(n+1)/2\\) points.\n\n2. **Trivial constructions.**  \n   *All vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\) cover \\(T_n\\); they are non\u2011sunny, so \\(k=0\\) is always possible.  \n   *Replace one of the vertical lines by any sunny line that passes through a point of that column; this yields a configuration with exactly one sunny line, proving that \\(k=1\\) is always possible.\n\n3. **Upper bound \u2013 impossibility of \\(k=2\\).**  \n   *Assume a configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists.  \n   *Let the \\(n-2\\) non\u2011sunny lines be vertical (the other two possibilities \u2013 horizontal or slope \\(-1\\) \u2013 cannot help because they would miss points in the first column).  \n   *Consequently the first \\(n-2\\) columns are covered completely by vertical lines, and the remaining two columns form the set  \n\n   \\[\n   S=\\{(n-1,1),(n-1,2),\\dots ,(n-1,n-2),\\;(n,1)\\},\n   \\]\n\n   which is a triangular set of size \\(2\\) (three points).  \n   *A sunny line cannot contain two points of \\(S\\): any two distinct points of \\(S\\) share either the same abscissa, the same ordinate, or the same sum of coordinates, and the line through such a pair would be respectively vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\) \u2013 all forbidden.  \n   *Hence each of the two sunny lines can cover at most one point of \\(S\\), leaving at least one point uncovered \u2013 a contradiction.  \n   *Thus **\\(k=2\\) is impossible for every \\(n\\ge3\\).*\n\n4. **Reduction to a triangular board.**  \n   *Fix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) and \\(k\\neq2\\).  \n   *Take the first \\(n-k\\) columns and cover each of them by a vertical line; these are the \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines.  \n   *The uncovered points form a right\u2011angled triangular board  \n\n   \\[\n   \\Delta_k=\\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le a\\le k,\\;1\\le b\\le k+1-a\\},\n   \\]\n\n   which has exactly \\(\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\) points.  \n   *It remains to exhibit **\\(k\\) sunny lines** that cover every point of \\(\\Delta_k\\).  \n   *The case \\(k=0,1\\) is already handled; the case \\(k\\ge3\\) is settled by the explicit construction below.\n\n5. **Construction for a triangular board \\(\\Delta_k\\) with \\(k\\ge3\\).**  \n   For \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) define the line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\;y = \\frac{i-1}{i}\\,x + \\Bigl(1-\\frac{i-1}{i}\\Bigr) .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   *The slope of \\(L_i\\) is \\(\\displaystyle m_i=\\frac{i-1}{i}\\).  \n     Since \\(i\\ge1\\), we have \\(m_i\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore every \\(L_i\\) is sunny.  \n   *For a column \\(a\\) (\\(1\\le a\\le k\\)) the point of \\(L_i\\) in that column is  \n\n   \\[\n   b_{i,a}=m_i a +\\bigl(1-m_i\\bigr)=\\frac{i-1}{i}\\,a+\\frac{1}{i}\n   =\\frac{(i-1)a+1}{i}.\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(a\\) and \\(i\\) are positive integers, the numerator \\((i-1)a+1\\) is congruent to \\(1\\pmod i\\); consequently \\(b_{i,a}\\) is an integer.  \n\n   *Moreover \\(1\\le b_{i,a}\\le k+1-a\\).  \n     Indeed,\n     \\[\n     b_{i,a}\\le\\frac{(i-1)a+ i}{i}=a+\\frac{a}{i}\\le a+ (k-a)=k+1-a,\n     \\]\n     where we used \\(i\\le k\\).  \n     The lower bound \\(b_{i,a}\\ge1\\) follows from \\((i-1)a+1\\ge i\\).\n\n   *Thus for every column \\(a\\) and every \\(i\\) the point \\((a,b_{i,a})\\) belongs to \\(\\Delta_k\\) and lies on \\(L_i\\).  \n     Conversely, if \\((a,b)\\in\\Delta_k\\) then \\(b\\) can be written uniquely as \\(\\displaystyle b=\\frac{(i-1)a+1}{i}\\) for the integer  \n\n     \\[\n     i = \\frac{a+1}{a-b+1},\n     \\tag{3}\n     \\]\n\n     which is an integer between \\(1\\) and \\(k\\) because \\(1\\le b\\le k+1-a\\).  \n     Hence each point of \\(\\Delta_k\\) lies on exactly one of the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\).\n\n   *Consequently the family \\(\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}\\) consists of \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines that together cover the whole triangular board \\(\\Delta_k\\).\n\n6. **Putting everything together.**  \n   *For any \\(n\\ge3\\) and any admissible \\(k\\) (i.e. \\(k=0,1\\) or \\(3\\le k\\le n\\)) we proceed as follows:  \n\n   - Use the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\) (non\u2011sunny).  \n   - On the remaining triangular board \\(\\Delta_k\\) place the sunny lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) given by (1).  \n\n   *All \\(n\\) lines are distinct, exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny, and every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) belongs to at least one of the lines.  \n\n7. **The case \\(n=3\\).**  \n   *When \\(n=3\\) we have only three lines in total.  \n   *The construction of step\u202f5 works for \\(k=3\\) but would require three sunny lines; a sunny line can contain at most one point from each column, so three sunny lines can cover at most three points of \\(T_3\\), whereas \\(|T_3|=6\\).  \n   *Hence for \\(n=3\\) the only possible values are \\(k=0\\) (three vertical lines) and \\(k=1\\) (two vertical lines together with one sunny line through a point of the third column).  \n   *The impossibility of \\(k=2\\) follows from the argument of step\u202f3, and \\(k=3\\) is ruled out by the counting observation just mentioned.\n\n**Conclusion.**  \nAll admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly those stated in the boxed formula above.  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1\\}, & n=3,\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,3,4,\\dots ,n\\}, & n\\ge 4 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]"
