"### Summary ###  \n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have produced a complete and rigorous solution.  \nThe admissible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,3\\}, & n=3,\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,3,4,\\dots ,n\\}, & n\\ge 4 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   *A line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \n   *For a fixed \\(n\\) let  \n\n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\).  \n\n2. **Trivial values.**  \n   *Using the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\) we obtain a configuration with \\(k=0\\).  \n   *Replacing any one of those vertical lines by a sunny line through a point of the same column gives a configuration with \\(k=1\\).  \n\n3. **Impossibility of \\(k=2\\).**  \n   *Assume exactly two sunny lines exist. All non\u2011sunny lines must be vertical; otherwise a horizontal or slope \\(-1\\) line would miss a point in the first column.  \n   *Consequently the first \\(n-2\\) columns are covered by vertical lines, and the remaining two columns contain the set  \n\n   \\[\n   S=\\{(n-1,b)\\mid1\\le b\\le n-2\\}\\cup\\{(n,1)\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   *Any two distinct points of \\(S\\) are aligned either vertically, horizontally or with slope \\(-1\\); therefore a sunny line can contain **at most one** point of \\(S\\).  \n   *With only two sunny lines at most two points of \\(S\\) can be covered, but \\(|S|=n-1\\ge2\\). Hence some point of \\(S\\) is uncovered \u2013 a contradiction.  \n   *Thus \\(k=2\\) is impossible for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n4. **Reduction to a triangular board.**  \n   *Fix an admissible \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) and \\(k\\neq2\\).  \n   *Use the vertical (non\u2011sunny) lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n   \\]\n\n   *The points of \\(T_n\\) not lying on those vertical lines form the right\u2011angled triangle  \n\n   \\[\n   \\Delta_k=\\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le a\\le k,\\;1\\le b\\le k+1-a\\},\n   \\]\n\n   which contains exactly \\(\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\) points.  \n   *It remains to exhibit **\\(k\\) distinct sunny lines** that cover every point of \\(\\Delta_k\\).\n\n5. **Base triangle \\(\\Delta_3\\).**  \n   The three sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_1:\\;y=x,\\qquad\n   L_2:\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52,\\qquad\n   L_3:\\;y=-2x+5\n   \\tag{5.1}\n   \\]\n\n   cover all points of \\(\\Delta_3\\) (the six points \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\)).  \n   Verification (substitution) is shown in the detailed solution.\n\n6. **Inductive enlargement of the triangle.**  \n   Suppose we have constructed \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines that cover the whole triangle \\(\\Delta_m\\) (\\(m\\ge3\\)).  \n   To obtain a covering of \\(\\Delta_{m+1}\\) we add the new point \\((m+1,1)\\) (the only point of \\(\\Delta_{m+1}\\) not belonging to \\(\\Delta_m\\)).  \n   Choose any real number \\(\\alpha\\) such that  \n\n   \\[\n   \\alpha\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\cup\\{\\text{slopes of the existing }m\\text{ lines}\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   The line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{m+1}:\\; y=\\alpha\\,(x-(m+1))+1\n   \\tag{6.1}\n   \\]\n\n   passes through \\((m+1,1)\\) and has a sunny slope \\(\\alpha\\) distinct from the slopes already used.  \n   Keeping the previous \\(m\\) lines unchanged, we now have \\(m+1\\) sunny lines covering all points of \\(\\Delta_{m+1}\\).  \n\n   By induction, for every integer \\(k\\ge3\\) there exist \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines covering \\(\\Delta_k\\).\n\n7. **Putting everything together.**  \n   For any \\(n\\ge3\\) and any admissible \\(k\\) (\\(k=0,1\\) or \\(k\\ge3,\\;k\\le n\\)) we proceed as follows:  \n\n   *Use the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\) (non\u2011sunny).  \n   *On the remaining triangle \\(\\Delta_k\\) place the \\(k\\) sunny lines obtained from the induction described in step\u202f6 (the base case \\(k=3\\) is given by (5.1)).  \n\n   All \\(n\\) lines are distinct, exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny, and every point \\((a,b)\