we need to find possible k.\n\nThe solution's approach: They first show that k=0 is possible using vertical lines x=1,...,x=n. Indeed, vertical lines cover all points because each column x = i for i=1..n includes points (i,b) with b ≤ n+1-i, which are exactly the points in column i. So vertical lines cover all points. So k=0 works.\n\nk=1: Replace one vertical line with a sunny line that passes through a point in that column. For example, replace x=i with line through (i,1) of slope 1 (i.e., line y = x - i + 1). That line is sunny (slope 1). Does it still cover all points? The replaced column's points are now covered by the sunny line? Let's check: The column x=i originally had points (i,b) for b from 1 to n+1-i. The sunny line y = x - i + 1 passes through (i,1). For b>1, does the line cover those points? No, the line only contains points where y = x - i + 1, i.e., points (i,1) only for that column. So the other points in column i (i,b) for b>1 are not covered. However, the solution says \"Replace any one of the lines in (2) by a sunny line that passes through a point of the same column (for instance, through (i,1) with slope 1). Exactly one line becomes sunny, the other n-1 remain vertical, and all points of T_n are still covered.\" This seems false: If we replace vertical line x=i with a sunny line that passes through (i,1) only, then points (i,2), (i,3), ... are uncovered. But maybe they rely on the fact that other vertical lines cover those points? No, those are in column i, so only vertical line x=i covered them originally. If we remove that vertical line, those points are uncovered. So k=1 construction is flawed.\n\nBut perhaps they intend to replace the vertical line x=i with a sunny line that passes through all points in that column? Could there be a sunny line that passes through all points in column i? No, because the column points have same x coordinate, so any line that passes through two points with same x coordinate is vertical (non-sunny). So cannot have a sunny line covering multiple points in the same column. So the claim that k=1 is always attainable is suspect.\n\nBut maybe they intend to replace vertical line x=i with a sunny line that passes through (i,1) and also some other points in other columns, and the other points in column i are covered by other lines? Wait, the other lines are vertical lines for other columns. But column i's points beyond (i,1) cannot be covered by other vertical lines because those have different x coordinate. So they would need to be covered by the sunny line. But a sunny line cannot be vertical, so cannot cover more than one point in column i (unless those points lie on a line with slope not 0,∞,-1). Let's examine points (i,b) for b>1. They have coordinates (i,b). Could there be a sunny line that passes through (i,b) and also some other points? Yes, but it would have slope (b - y2)/(i - x2) for some other point (x2,y2). But to also cover (i,1) we need a line through (i,1) and (i,b) which is vertical, slope ∞, not sunny. So we cannot have a single sunny line covering both (i,1) and (i,b). So we need separate lines for each point in column i beyond (i,1). But we have only one sunny line to replace the vertical line. So we cannot cover all points in column i. So the claim that k=1 is always attainable is false.\n\nThus the solution's answer is likely incorrect. However, maybe there is a different construction for k=1 that they didn't describe fully? Let's think: Could we have n lines, exactly one sunny, covering all points? Perhaps we can use n-1 vertical lines and one sunny line that is not vertical, but also some horizontal or slope -1 lines? Wait, k=1 means exactly one sunny line, the rest (n-1) must be non-sunny (i.e., horizontal, vertical, or slope -1). The solution used vertical lines for the rest. But maybe we can use a combination of vertical and horizontal lines to cover all points while using only one sunny line. For instance, we could use vertical lines for columns 1..n-