ally (1,1) is covered by vertical line x=1. So we need to cover points (1,2),(1,3),(1,4) all in column 1, plus (2,1),(2,2) in column 2, plus (3,1). Could we use a sunny line that passes through (1,2) and (2,1)? Slope (1-2)/(2-1) = -1, not allowed. (1,2) and (2,2) slope (2-2)/(2-1) = 0, not allowed. (1,2) and (3,1) slope (1-2)/(3-1) = -0.5, which is allowed (sunny). So line through (1,2) and (3,1) slope -0.5, sunny. That line also passes through (2,1.5) which is not a lattice point. So it covers (1,2) and (3,1). Then we need to cover (1,3),(1,4),(2,1),(2,2). Could we use a sunny line covering (1,3) and (2,1)? Slope (1-3)/(2-1) = -2, allowed. That line passes through (1,3) and (2,1). It also passes through (3,-1) outside. So covers (1,3) and (2,1). Then we still need (1,4) and (2,2). Could we use a sunny line covering (1,4) and (2,2)? Slope (2-4)/(2-1) = -2, same slope as previous line, but passes through different points. However, we only have two sunny lines left (we already used two: line through (1,2)-(3,1) and line through (1,3)-(2,1)). Actually we used two sunny lines already, so we cannot add a third. So we need to cover all remaining points with those two lines, which seems impossible because we have four points left. Could we adjust the lines to cover more points? Perhaps we can use a sunny line that passes through three points? Let's see if any three of the remaining points are collinear with a sunny slope. Points (1,2),(2,2),(3,2) are horizontal, not allowed. (1,2),(2,3),(3,4) would be slope 1, but (2,3) is covered by slope -1 line? Actually (2,3) is covered by slope -1 line x+y=5, so we don't need to cover it. So we need to cover (1,2),(1,3),(1,4),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1). Could we use a sunny line that passes through (1,4) and (2,2) slope -2, and also passes through (3,0) which is not needed. That covers (1,4) and (2,2). Could we also use a sunny line that passes through (1,3) and (2,1) slope -2, but that's same slope as previous line but different intercept. So