vered by three sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Inductive construction for larger triangles\n\nAssume that for some \\(m\\ge3\\) we have already constructed \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines\n\\(\\mathcal L_m=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_m\\}\\) covering the whole triangle \\(\\Delta_m\\).\n\nThe triangle \\(\\Delta_{m+1}\\) contains exactly one additional point,\n\\((m+1,1)\\) (all other points already belong to \\(\\Delta_m\\)).\nChoose a real number \\(\\alpha\\) satisfying  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\cup\\{\\text{slope}(L_i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le m\\}.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nSuch an \\(\\alpha\\) exists because the set on the right‑hand side is finite.\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nL_{m+1}:\\; y=\\alpha\\bigl(x-(m+1)\\bigr)+1 .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(L_{m+1}\\) passes through \\((m+1,1)\\), has sunny slope \\(\\alpha\\), and its slope is different from those of the lines in \\(\\mathcal L_m\\); consequently the \\(m+1\\) lines  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{m+1}= \\mathcal L_m\\cup\\{L_{m+1}\\}\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nare distinct and all sunny.\nSince the previous \\(m\\) lines still cover \\(\\Delta_m\\) and the new line covers the only new point, \\(\\mathcal L_{m+1}\\) covers \\(\\Delta_{m+1}\\).\n\nBy induction, for every integer \\(k\\ge3\\) there exist \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines covering \\(\\Delta_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Completion of the original problem\n\nLet \\(n\\ge3\\) and let \\(k\\) satisfy (4).  \nProceed as follows:\n\n* Use the vertical lines (5) – they are non‑sunny and number \\(n-k\\).  \n* On the triangle \\(\\Delta_k\\) place the \\(k\\) sunny lines obtained from the induction of Sections 4–5 (the base case \\(k=3\\) is given by (7)).\n\nAll \\(n\\) lines are distinct, exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny, and every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line (either on a vertical line or on the appropriate sunny line).  \n\nThus a configurati