n cover at most one point of \\(S\\), at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S\\) can be covered.  \n   But \\(|S|=n-1\\) and we already used two of the \\(n\\) lines for the verticals (3.1).  \n   Hence only \\(n-2\\) lines remain to cover the \\(n-1\\) points of \\(S\\), which is impossible:\n\n   \\[\n   n-2 < n-1\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\\text{some point of }S\\text{ is uncovered}.\n   \\]\n\n   This contradiction shows that a configuration with exactly two sunny lines cannot exist for any \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n4. **Construction for all admissible \\(k\\ge3\\)** –  \n   *Step 4.1* (vertical backbone).  \n   For a prescribed \\(k\\) (\\(3\\le k\\le n\\)) use the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n   \\tag{4.1}\n   \\]\n\n   The points of \\(T_n\\) not lying on those verticals form the right‑angled triangle  \n\n   \\[\n   \\Delta_k=\\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le a\\le k,\\;1\\le b\\le k+1-a\\},\n   \\tag{4.2}\n   \\]\n\n   which contains exactly \\(\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\) points.\n\n   *Step 4.2* (base triangle \\(\\Delta_3\\)).  \n   The three sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_1:\\;y=x,\\qquad\n   L_2:\\;y=-\\frac12x+\\frac52,\\qquad\n   L_3:\\;y=-2x+5\n   \\tag{4.3}\n   \\]\n\n   have slopes \\(1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\) (all sunny) and cover the six points of \\(\\Delta_3\\):\n   \\[\n   (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1).\n   \\]\n\n   *Step 4.3* (inductive enlargement).  \n   Assume that for some \\(m\\ge3\\) we have constructed \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines covering \\(\\Delta_m\\).  \n   The triangle \\(\\Delta_{m+1}\\) adds exactly one new point, namely \\((m+1,1)\\).  \n   Choose a real number \\(\\alpha\\) such that  \n\n   \\[\n   \\alpha\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\cup\\{\\text{slope of the existing }m\\text{ lines}\\}.\n   \\tag{4.4}\n   \\]\n\n   (the set on the right‑hand side is finite, so such an \\(\\alpha\\) exists).  \n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{m+1}:\\;y=\\alpha\\bigl(x-(m+1)\\bigr)+1 .\n   \\tag{4.5}\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(L_{m+1}\\) passes t