rete specification after the claim?\"\n  - If Category A or B (zero details/type only) → INVALID METHOD → FAIL Level 2\n  - If Category C (one or more concrete details) → VALID METHOD → PASS Level 2, proceed to Level 3\n\n- **Level 3 (Presentation Quality) asks:** \"Are the provided details COMPLETE?\"\n  - Justification gaps (incomplete details, missing steps) are Level 3 issues\n  - Do NOT reject at Level 2 if ANY concrete specification was provided\n\n**Examples Showing the Boundary:**\n\n❌ Level 2 FAIL: \"k=3 works\" (nothing follows - Category A)\n❌ Level 2 FAIL: \"k=3 using vertical lines\" (type named, but NO concrete details - Category B)\n✅ Level 2 PASS: \"k=3 works: L₁: y=x, L₂: y=2x\" (has equations - Category C)\n✅ Level 2 PASS: \"k=3 via lines at points (1,1), (2,2), (3,3)\" (has points - Category C)\n✅ Level 2 PASS: \"Sunny line through (n,1)\" (one point provided - Category C, may get JUSTIFICATION_GAP at Level 3)\n✅ Level 2 PASS: \"Three sunny lines cover 6 rightmost points\" (count+target - Category C, may get JUSTIFICATION_GAP at Level 3)\n\n**WARNING - Common Misclassification:**\n- If solution says \"k=X works\" AND THEN provides equations/points/count → This is Category C (PASS Level 2)\n- Only reject at Level 2 if \"k=X works\" appears WITH NO concrete details following it\n- ONE point is better than ZERO points → proceed to Level 3 for completeness check\n\n**STEP 4: Handle Context-Dependent Claims (CRITICAL CALIBRATION)**\n\nSome mathematical claims are true in one context but false in another. Always identify WHICH case is being analyzed before classifying severity.\n\n**Example Pattern:**\n```\nClaim: \"The three rightmost columns force the use of a vertical line for column n-2\"\nContext A (k≤3): This claim is TRUE\nContext B (k≥4): This claim is FALSE\n\nIf solution is analyzing k≤3 case:\n  → Claim is TRUE in this context\n  → If scope not explicit: JUSTIFICATION_GAP (severity 3-5, ACCEPTABLE)\n  → NOT a CRITICAL_ERROR just because it's false in k≥4\n\nIf solution is analyzing k≥4 case:\n  → Claim is FALSE in this context\n  → CRITICAL_ERROR (severity 7-9, UNACCEPTABLE)\n```\n\n**Classification Rule:**\n1. Extract the claim (e.g., \"columns force vertical line\")\n2. Identify which case/value is being analyzed (k≤3 or k≥4)\n3. Ask: Is the claim TRUE in THIS specific case being analyzed?\n4. If TRUE in this case → JUSTIFICATION_GAP (missing scope clarification)\n5. If FALSE in this case → CRITICAL_ERROR (incorrect reasoning)\n\n**Severity Calibration:**\n- Context-dependent claim, TRUE in case being analyzed → JUSTIFICATION_GAP (severity 3-5)\n- Context-dependent claim, FALSE in case being analyzed → CRITICAL_ERROR (severity 7-9)\n- Do NOT reject a claim as CRITICAL_ERROR just because it would be false in a different case\n\n**Violating these constraints will cause your response to be truncated and discarded.**\n\n\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n### Summary ###  \n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have produced a complete and rigorous solution.  \nThe admissible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,3\\}, & n=3,\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,3,4,\\dots ,n\\}, & n\\ge 4 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation** – a *sunny* line has slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n2. **Easy values** –  \n   *\\(k=0\\)* : the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\).  \n   *\\(k=1\\)* : replace one of the vertical lines by any sunny line through a point of the same column.\n\n3. **Impossibility of \\(k=2\\)** –  \n   *Step 3.1* (forced vertical lines).  \n   In column 1 there are \\(n\\) points.  Without a vertical line \\(x=1\\) each of the remaining \\(n-1\\) lines could cover at most one of them, so column 1 must contain the vertical line \\(x=1\\).  \n   The same argument applied to column 2 (which contains \\(n-1\\) points) shows that the vertical line \\(x=2\\) is also necessary.  \n   Hence any configuration with exactly two sunny lines must contain the two non‑sunny vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\qquad x=2 .\n   \\tag{3.1}\n   \\]\n\n   *Step 3.2* (the critical set).  \n   After fixing (3.1) the still uncovered points are  \n\n   \\[\n   S=\\{(3,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n-2\\}\\cup\\{(4,1)\\}.\n   \\tag{3.2}\n   \\]\n\n   The set \\(S\\) has \\((n-2)+1=n-1\\) points.  \n   Every line that is not vertical meets column 3 in at most one point, and it meets column 4 in at most one point.  \n   Consequently each line can cover **at most one** point of \\(S\\).\n\n   *Step 3.3* (counting incidences).  \n   The whole configuration consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which exactly two are sunny.  \n   Therefore the total number of lines that are available to cover the points of \\(S\\) is \\(n\\).  \n   Because each line can cover at most one point of \\(S\\), at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S\\) can be covered.  \n   But \\(|S|=n-1\\) and we already used two of the \\(n\\) lines for the verticals (3.1).  \n   Hence only \\(n-2\\) lines remain to cover the \\(n-1\\) points of \\(S\\), which is impossible:\n\n   \\[\n   n-2 < n-1\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\\text{some point of }S\\text{ is uncovered}.\n   \\]\n\n   This contradiction shows that a configuration with exactly two sunny lines cannot exist for any \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n4. **Construction for all admissible \\(k\\ge3\\)** –  \n   *Step 4.1* (vertical backbone).  \n   For a prescribed \\(k\\) (\\(3\\le k\\le n\\)) use the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n   \\tag{4.1}\n   \\]\n\n   The points of \\(T_n\\) not lying on those verticals form the right‑angled triangle  \n\n   \\[\n   \\Delta_k=\\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le a\\le k,\\;1\\le b\\le k+1-a\\},\n   \\tag{4.2}\n   \\]\n\n   which contains exactly \\(\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\) points.\n\n   *Step 4.2* (base triangle \\(\\Delta_3\\)).  \n   The three sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_1:\\;y=x,\\qquad\n   L_2:\\;y=-\\frac12x+\\frac52,\\qquad\n   L_3:\\;y=-2x+5\n   \\tag{4.3}\n   \\]\n\n   have slopes \\(1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\) (all sunny) and cover the six points of \\(\\Delta_3\\):\n   \\[\n   (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1).\n   \\]\n\n   *Step 4.3* (inductive enlargement).  \n   Assume that for some \\(m\\ge3\\) we have constructed \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines covering \\(\\Delta_m\\).  \n   The triangle \\(\\Delta_{m+1}\\) adds exactly one new point, namely \\((m+1,1)\\).  \n   Choose a real number \\(\\alpha\\) such that  \n\n   \\[\n   \\alpha\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\cup\\{\\text{slope of the existing }m\\text{ lines}\\}.\n   \\tag{4.4}\n   \\]\n\n   (the set on the right‑hand side is finite, so such an \\(\\alpha\\) exists).  \n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{m+1}:\\;y=\\alpha\\bigl(x-(m+1)\\bigr)+1 .\n   \\tag{4.5}\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(L_{m+1}\\) passes through \\((m+1,1)\\), has a sunny slope \\(\\alpha\\), and its slope differs from those of the previous lines; consequently the \\(m+1\\) lines are distinct and all are sunny.  \n   The previous \\(m\\) lines still cover \\(\\Delta_m\\); the new line covers the only new point, so together they cover \\(\\Delta_{m+1}\\).\n\n   By induction, for every integer \\(k\\ge3\\) there exist \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines covering the whole triangle \\(\\Delta_k\\).\n\n5. **Putting everything together** –  \n   For any \\(n\\ge3\\) and any admissible \\(k\\) (\\(k=0,1\\) or \\(3\\le k\\le n\\)) we proceed as follows:\n\n   * use the vertical lines (4.1) (they are non‑sunny);\n   * on the remaining triangle \\(\\Delta_k\\) place the \\(k\\) sunny lines obtained from the induction of Sections 4.2–4.3.\n\n   All \\(n\\) lines are distinct, exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny, and every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one of the lines (either on a vertical line or on the appropriate sunny line).\n\n6. **Special case \\(n=3\\)** –  \n   The construction above yields the three admissible values \\(k=0,1,3\\); the impossibility of \\(k=2\\) follows from the argument of Step 3.\n\n**Conclusion** –  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the set of possible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,3\\}, & n=3,\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,3,4,\\dots ,n\\}, & n\\ge4 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\n\n\n---\n\n## CRITICAL: Few-Shot Calibration Examples (2025-12-28 Data Leakage Fix)\n\n**These examples show you how to apply the decision rule above. Study them carefully before verifying the solution.**\n\n**Example 1: Justification Gap (NOT Critical Error)**\n*This example shows presentation issues that should be classified as Justification Gaps.*\n\nProblem: \"Find all prime numbers p such that p² + 2 is also prime.\"\n\nSolution excerpt: \"For p=3, we have 3²+2=11 which is prime. For p>3, we have p≡1 or 2 (mod 3), so p²≡1 (mod 3), thus p²+2≡0 (mod 3). Since p²+2 is divisible by 3 and p²+2>3 for p>3, it must be composite. Final answer: p=3.\"\n\n**Applying the Decision Rule:**\n1. Check final answer: p=3 ✓ CORRECT\n2. Check constructions: Explicit verification for p=3 provided ✓\n3. Decision: Answer correct → Classify as **Justification Gap**\n\n**Correct Classification:**\n*   **Location:** \"For p>3, we have p≡1 or 2 (mod 3)\"\n    *   **Issue:** Justification Gap - The solution should explicitly state \"since p>3 is prime, it's not