t if we use vertical lines x=1 and x=4? x=4 only covers (4,1). Then we need to cover remaining points: column 2 (2,1),(2,2),(2,3); column 3 (3,1),(3,2); column 1 points except (1,1)-(1,4) are covered by x=1. So we need to cover (2,1),(2,2),(2,3),(3,1),(3,2) with two sunny lines. That's 5 points. Could we cover them? Let's see if any sunny line can cover multiple points. For example, line through (2,1) and (3,2) slope = (1-2)/(2-3) = -1/(-1) = 1, which is sunny (slope 1). So L1: line through (2,1) and (3,2) (slope 1) covers those two points. Then we need to cover (2,2),(2,3),(3,1) with one sunny line. Let's see if any line can cover all three. (2,2) and (3,1) slope = (2-1)/(2-3) = 1/(-1) = -1, not allowed. (2,2) and (2,3) vertical, not allowed. (2,3) and (3,1) slope = (3-1)/(2-3) = 2/(-1) = -2, which is sunny (slope -2). So line through (2,3) and (3,1) slope -2 is sunny. That line covers (2,3) and (3,1). It does not cover (2,2). So we still have (2,2) uncovered. Could we adjust? Maybe we can choose different lines. Let's try to cover (2,2) with one of the lines. For example, line through (2,2) and (3,1) slope = (2-1)/(2-3) = 1/(-1) = -1, not allowed. (2,2) and (3,2) slope = (2-2)/(2-3) = 0, not allowed (horizontal). (2,2) and (2,3) vertical, not allowed. So (2,2) cannot be paired with any of the other points to form a sunny line. So (2,2) must be covered by a line that only covers that point. But we have only two sunny lines total. So we cannot cover all points. So k=2 impossible for n=4.\n\nThus the impossibility of k=2 seems plausible.\n\nNow for k≥3, the solution provides a construction using vertical lines for columns 1 to n-k, and then constructing k sunny lines covering the triangle Δ_k (the right triangle of size k). The construction for k≥3 is plausible: they construct k sunny lines covering Δ_k via induction: start with k=3 using three specific sunny lines covering Δ_3 (the triangle of points (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)). Then for each step, they add