n lines that cover all points (a,b) with a,b positive integers and a+b ≤ n+1. The set of points is a triangular region of integer lattice points: T_n = {(a,b) : a,b ≥ 1, a+b ≤ n+1}. There are N = n(n+1)/2 points.\n\nWe have n lines, each line can be any line (including vertical/horizontal or slope -1 lines). The lines must be distinct. Exactly k of them are sunny (i.e., slopes not equal to 0, ∞, -1). The rest n-k lines are non-sunny: they can be vertical (x = const), horizontal (y = const), or slope -1 (x + y = const). But note that horizontal lines are allowed? The problem says \"not parallel to any of the x-axis, the y-axis, and the line x+y=0\". So non-sunny lines are parallel to x-axis (horizontal), y-axis (vertical), or line x+y=0 (i.e., slope -1). So yes.\n\nWe need to find all possible k for which such a configuration exists.\n\nThe solution constructs for k=0: n vertical lines x=1,...,x=n. Does that cover all points? Let's check: Points (a,b) with a+b ≤ n+1. For any such point, a ∈ [1,n], b ∈ [1,n+1-a]. The vertical line x=a passes through all points with that a coordinate. So vertical lines x=1,...,x=n cover all points. Indeed, we need n lines, we have n vertical lines, all non-sunny. So k=0 works for any n.\n\nk=1: n-1 vertical lines x=1,...,x=n-1, plus one sunny line that passes through a point in column x=n. That covers all points: column x=n has points (n,b) where b ≤ 1 (since a+b ≤ n+1 => b ≤ 1). Actually for a=n, b can be 1 only (since b≥1). So only point (n,1). So we need a line covering (n,1). The sunny line passes through (n,1) and can be any line not vertical/horizontal/slope -1. It will also potentially cover other points, but that's okay. So k=1 works.\n\nk=n: all lines are sunny. The solution constructs for any k≥3: use n-k vertical lines (cover columns 1 to n-k) and then cover the remaining points (a,b) with a > n-k via k sunny lines covering the triangle Δ_k defined as points with a ∈ [1,k], b ∈ [1,k+1-a]. Wait: Let's examine the construction