 can achieve any k≥3 up to n. So for n≥4, we can achieve k=0,1,3,...,n.\n\nNow we need to check if k=2 is impossible for n≥4. The solution gives an impossibility proof: assume exactly two sunny lines exist. Then they argue that columns 1 and 2 must be covered by vertical lines x=1 and x=2. Then they count uncovered points, and argue that each remaining line can cover at most one point in column 3, so we need at least n-2 lines to cover column 3, which uses up all remaining lines, leaving no lines to cover other columns. So impossible.\n\nWe need to check the correctness of this argument.\n\nLet's examine the argument carefully.\n\nWe have n lines, exactly two are sunny, the rest n-2 are non-sunny (vertical, horizontal, or slope -1). They argue:\n\n- Column 1 (points (1,b) for b=1..n) has n points. Non-vertical non-sunny lines (horizontal or slope -1) meet column 1 at most one point each. Sunny lines also meet column 1 at most one point each (since they are not vertical). So total points covered by non-vertical lines (including sunny) is at most 2 (by the two sunny lines) + (n-2) non-vertical non-sunny lines each covering at most one point. But they argue that we need to cover n points, so we need at least one vertical line covering at least two points. Actually they argue that the only non-sunny line that can meet column 1 in more than one point is vertical line x=1. So we need x=1.\n\nBut we have n-2 non-sunny lines. Some could be vertical, some horizontal, some slope -1. The argument says: \"Since we have only two sunny lines, at most two points of column 1 could be covered by sunny lines; the remaining n-2 points must be covered by non-sunny lines. The only non-sunny line that can meet column 1 in more than one point is the vertical line x=1. Hence x=1 must belong to the configuration.\" This seems correct: we need to cover n points in column 1. The two sunny lines can each cover at most one point in column 1 (since they are not vertical). So they can cover at most 2 points. The remaining n-2 points must be covered by the n-2 non-sunny lines. If any of those non-sunny lines is not vertical, it can cover at most one point in column 1. So to cover n-2 points, we need at least n-2 non-vertical non-sunny lines covering at most n-2 points (one each). But we have exactly n-2 non-sunny lines. So it's possible that each non-sunny line is non-vertical and covers exactly one point. That would cover n-2 points, plus the two sunny lines covering 2 points, total n points. So we wouldn't need a vertical line. Wait, the argument says that the only non-sunny line that can meet column 1 in more than one point is vertical. But we don't need a line that meets column 1 in more than one point; we need to cover n points. If we have n-2 non-sunny lines each covering one point, plus two sunny lines covering one point each, we get n points, which is enough. So the argument that we must have x=1 is not strictly necessary; we could have all non-sunny lines be non-vertical, each covering a distinct point in column 1, and the two sunny lines covering the remaining two points. That would cover all points in column 1. So the claim that x=1 must be present is not forced.\n\nBut we need to consider the nature of non-sunny lines: they can be horizontal (y=const) or slope -1 (x+y=const). Horizontal lines y=c intersect column 1 at point (1,c). So each horizontal line covers exactly one point in column 1. Similarly, slope -1 lines x+y=c intersect column 1 at point (1,c-1). So also exactly one point. So indeed, each non-sunny line can cover at most one point in column 1. So we could have n-2 distinct non-sunny lines covering n-2 distinct points in column 1. That would be possible if we have enough distinct horizontal or slope -1 lines. However, we have constraints: the lines must be distinct, but we can have up to n-2 distinct horizontal lines (each with different y intercept) or slope -1 lines (different constant). So it's possible to have n-2 horizontal lines